[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate · 1:30] Let me take a second to structure this.

Since revenue is actually up 15% while margin fell from 24% to 6%, that's an 18-point swing — this smells like a cost problem rather than a demand problem, but I want to confirm the revenue growth is healthy before I assume that.

So I'd break it into three buckets. First, revenue quality: is the 15% coming from new store openings, same-store traffic, or price increases? Growth from new stores can mask flat or declining per-store performance. Second, cost structure: split COGS from operating expenses, and within each, find which line grew faster than revenue. For a coffee chain I'd expect COGS to be coffee, dairy, cups, and food, and opex to be labor, rent, and utilities. Third, external factors: commodity prices, wage inflation, lease renewals.

Can I start with the revenue side — do we know how much of the 15% is new locations versus existing ones?
