[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:32] Okay. Is that structure MECE, and if not, what's missing? Then tell me which branch you'd prioritize, and why. On what you asked for: 200 stores today — and it was 200 stores two years ago, so the footprint hasn’t changed. On the data you asked for — I'll come to that shortly.

[candidate · 3:02] Fairly MECE on the P&L side, but my third bucket overlaps the first two — commodity prices and wages are drivers of cost lines, not a separate branch. Cleaner version: revenue = price × volume, cost = fixed vs. variable, and then drivers sit underneath each. I'm also missing anything below operating income — interest, depreciation from a remodel program, taxes — which could absolutely explain margin without touching operations.

I'd prioritize costs. With a flat 200-store footprint, the 15% is same-store growth, so volume or ticket is up and the business is selling more while keeping less. That's almost definitionally a cost-per-unit story.

One thing I'd want: has the client raised menu prices in that window? If the 15% is pure traffic with flat pricing, input inflation would hit margin directly with no offset.
