[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 3:05] Menu prices have not changed in two years. Average revenue per store is $2.4M a year. Okay. Which cost line would you want to look at first, and why?

[candidate · 4:35] COGS first — specifically the dairy and coffee inputs, since those are the volume-driven lines and volume is clearly up with prices flat.

Let me size it. 200 stores × $2.4M = $480M in revenue today. An 18-point margin drop on that base is roughly $86M of profit gone, and with no price change, essentially all of it has to show up as cost growth outrunning the 15% revenue growth.

For a specialty coffee chain I'd expect COGS around 30–35% of revenue and labor in the high 20s. If COGS was at the low end two years ago and absorbed most of that $86M, it would now be sitting somewhere near 48–50% of revenue — call it a 15-point jump. Is that roughly the magnitude the data shows on the COGS line, or is the damage split more evenly with labor?
