[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 7:42] Your arithmetic is consistent now. Here's what the inputs show, so you can test your pairing against it. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Where does your dairy-driven story stand against these?

[candidate · 9:12] My dairy story doesn't hold — this is broad-based, not one input.

Checking it: beans at 25% of 42 points = 10.5 points, up 40% → 14.7, so +4.2. Everything else is 31.5 points, up 37.5% → 43.3, so +11.8. Total 58.0 points, which reconciles exactly to the reported COGS line.

So the whole 16-point move is explained by input inflation at roughly 38% across the board — beans and non-bean inputs moved almost identically. There's no single culprit to renegotiate away, and no evidence of waste, shrink, or portion creep, since the math closes without any residual.

The real root cause, then, isn't that costs rose — it's that the client absorbed 38% input inflation with zero price movement in two years. That's a pricing failure as much as a cost shock.

One thing I'd want before recommending: does the 15% revenue growth come from more transactions or a higher average ticket? Is that available?
