[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 9:15] The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit. Take a look at the exhibit, and tell me what it adds to your view. Then, beyond pricing, what else could the client do?

[candidate · 10:45] The exhibit says this is a steady bleed, not a shock — COGS moved 42 → 50 → 58, a clean 8 points a year, and profit 24 → 15 → 6. If nothing changes, next year the chain is roughly break-even to negative. That urgency matters for the recommendation.

Also worth noting the 15%: ticket is up 10%, so transactions are only up about 5%. And the ticket growth is attach rate, not price — customers buying more items. That's actually unfavorable, because every incremental item carries the inflated 58% cost.

Beyond pricing, three levers. One, menu mix — food and packaging-heavy items are likely the worst offenders; push customers toward high-margin espresso drinks rather than just more items. Two, procurement — forward contracts or hedging on beans and dairy, which they clearly don't have if they ate 38% spot inflation. Three, portion and waste standardization, plus reviewing whether low-margin SKUs should be cut entirely.
