[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 10:47] Okay. Which of those levers, together with pricing, would you rank first for the CEO, and why?

[candidate · 12:17] Pricing, clearly first — and it's not close.

The arithmetic: to restore the old 24% margin, COGS needs to come back from 58 points to 42. If I hold input costs fixed and only raise prices, I need revenue to grow enough that the same dollar costs are 42% of the new base — 58/42 = 1.38, so about a 38% price increase. That's not realistic in one move, but it frames the gap. A more modest 10% increase takes COGS from 58 to about 53 points and lifts margin roughly 5 points — meaningful, immediate, and it requires no supplier negotiation or operational change.

Procurement second, because hedging stops the bleed from continuing at 8 points a year, but it doesn't recover what's already lost.

Menu mix third — real, but slower and harder to measure.

My main risk on pricing is elasticity. Transactions are only up 5%, so traffic isn't especially strong. I'd pilot in a subset of the 200 stores before a national rollout.
