[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 12:19] Okay. Put that together as your recommendation to the CEO: what do you tell them to do, in what order, and what do you watch to know it's working?

[candidate · 13:49] My recommendation: Brew & Bean should raise menu prices immediately — that's the root cause and the fastest fix.

The reasoning: COGS went from 42 to 58 points of revenue, which accounts for 16 of the 18 points of margin loss, and that's entirely input inflation of about 38% across beans, dairy, packaging, and food. Labor is flat and overhead moved only 2 points. The client has not raised prices in two years, so they absorbed all of it.

Sequencing. First, a 10–12% price increase, piloted across maybe 20 stores for one quarter, then rolled out — that recovers roughly 5 points of margin. Second, lock in procurement with forward contracts so the 8-points-a-year bleed stops. Third, rework menu mix toward high-margin drinks over food.

What I'd watch: transaction count in pilot stores versus control, to catch elasticity; attach rate, since more items at 58% cost isn't actually helping; and COGS as a percent of revenue monthly.

Biggest risk is traffic — transactions are only up 5%, so demand isn't robust. If pilot traffic drops more than about 5%, I'd step the increase down and lean harder on procurement.
